\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Dictator Games and The Notion of Integrity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 21, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

My band director from high school had a profound impact on my life beacuse
 he not only fostered my love of music, but he taught me important life
 lessons as well.
 On most days, he would relay thought provoking anecdotes and quotes, such
 as: 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Integrity is doing the right thing when no one is watching.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 This particular quote has inspired my consideration of the following question:
 Under what conditions will people follow the social norms associated with
 decent, honest behavior? The dictator game provides an excellent design
 to address some of the issues associated with this question.
 Levitt and List (2007) discuss the moral cost of behaving selfishly in
 such games.
 Furthermore, List (2007) designs an experiment that exogenously varies
 the action set and the origin of the endowment.
 He does this in order to analyze an exogenous increase in the moral cost
 of taking.
 His result, which is consonant with the results in List and Cherry (2007),
 highlights that earning the initial endowment versus simply receiving it
 causes a number of dictators to abstain from taking.
 With my experimental design, I hope to continue the analysis started by
 the three papers mentioned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the standard baseline dictator game, List (2007) had participants randomly
 assigned to two groups, one placed in room A and the other placed in room
 B.
 The two groups did not have any contact before, during, or after the session,
 and no subject participated in more than one treatment.
 Players in both groups are allocated an equal amount of money.
 Further, dictators are allocated an additional set of funds, and in a one-shot
 allocation game, dictators could allocate an amount of this endowment to
 their randomly determined partner in the other group.
 The chosen allocation determined final earnings.
 Now, two of the treatments List (2007) considered allowed subjects in room
 A the opportunity to take either $1 or up to $5, respectively, from room
 B subjects.
 This permitted an exploration of whether simply allowing taking can yield
 differences in behavior.
 Finally, he evaluated an earnings treatment, which was identical to his
 latter treatment, except subjects earned their monies.
 This is the treatment I would like my research to expand upon.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moreover, I would like to analyze the effect of an additional treatment.
 In most dictator games, the right to be the first mover is usally given
 to the subjects who were allocated an additional amount of money, whether
 this money was earned or simply distributed.
 Instead, I would like to test the effects of reversing the allocation decision
 by granting the right to be first mover to the low earners.
 So, my experiment will consist of a 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 factorial design, which is represented by the following table:
\end_layout

